  var NEWS = [{"title": "The 4 best things to buy at Walmart this weekend", "category": "Everyday Stuff", "slug": "walmart-weekend-picks", "body": ["Walmart has a strong weekend lineup, and the four picks below are the ones Lucy would actually buy. First, the Ninja Creami, which turns a frozen base into ice cream, sorbet or milkshakes at home. Second, the Stanley Quencher tumbler, the Pond's first deal and still the one Lucy reaches for most. Third, a LEGO set for a rainy afternoon — the Millennium Falcon is the current favourite. Fourth, a CeraVe skincare set, because a good everyday routine should not cost a fortune.", "Prices change at the store, so check the price on the product page before you buy. The deals page lists the current price, the was-price and how much you save for each of these picks, with a link to the store. If a price has moved since Lucy checked it on November 15, 2026, the store price is the one that counts.", "Lucy only lists deals she would buy herself or gift to a friend. Some links are affiliate links, and if you shop through them she may earn a small commission. It never costs you extra, and it keeps the Pond swimming."]}, {"title": "What happened when I gave the AI-generated slop an honest try", "category": "Everyday Stuff", "slug": "ai-slop-honest-try", "body": ["AI content is here to stay. It is not perfect, but it is getting better. Lucy spent a week trying it out for recipes, shopping lists and news summaries, and the results were mixed in a useful way. The drafts were fast and often sensible, but they needed a human to check the prices, the ingredients and the facts. A recipe that looks right can still miss a step, and a deal that looks real can be out of date.", "The lesson from the week was simple: use the AI as a starting point, not as the final word. Check the store price, read the ingredients, and trust your own taste. That is how the Pond works too — every deal is checked before it goes live, and prices are refreshed every morning.", "Lucy did not cry. She did laugh, once, at a recipe that suggested freezing a sandwich. She will keep trying new tools, and she will keep telling you honestly what worked and what did not."]}, {"title": "Stocks rally as tech giants report strong earnings", "category": "Stocks", "slug": "stocks-rally-tech-earnings", "body": ["Major indexes climbed this week as tech companies posted better-than-expected results. Investors are feeling optimistic about the year ahead, and the gains were broad rather than limited to one company. Technology led the move, with strong results across software, devices and cloud services.", "Analysts caution that one good week does not make a trend. Earnings season still has further to run, and interest rates, jobs data and consumer spending will all matter in the weeks ahead. For now, though, the mood in the market is brighter than it was a month ago.", "This is a short news summary, not financial advice. If you are saving or investing, the Pond's rule is the same as its shopping rule: check the facts, take your time, and do not rush into something because everyone else is."]}, {"title": "Congress debates new infrastructure bill", "category": "Politics", "slug": "congress-infrastructure-bill", "body": ["Lawmakers are working on a major spending package that could reshape roads, bridges and broadband across the country. Supporters say the investment is overdue and would create jobs in every state. Critics say the cost is too high and want clearer limits on spending.", "The bill is still being debated, and the final version may look different from the draft. Key questions include how much goes to repairs versus new projects, how rural broadband is funded, and how the spending is paid for. Votes are expected in the coming weeks.", "The Pond Post will keep the summary short and plain. When the bill passes or changes, this article will be updated with what actually happened, not just what was proposed."]}, {"title": "South Korea's K-pop industry hits new records", "category": "South Korea", "slug": "kpop-records", "body": ["K-pop exports reached an all-time high this year, with groups selling out stadiums around the world. The Hallyu wave shows no signs of slowing down, and Korean music, drama and food are reaching new audiences in new countries. Concerts, albums and merchandise all contributed to the record.", "For fans, the year has brought bigger tours, more dates outside Asia, and more ways to follow their favourite groups. For the industry, the challenge is keeping that growth going while looking after the artists who make it happen.", "Lucy is learning Korean, so this story is a favourite in the Pond. If you are planning a trip to Seoul, the Travel page has a starter trip and a packing list, and the Korean Corner has ten phrases to learn before you go."]}];
